---
const ranges = [
  'Early SaaS: $300–$3,000/month in avoidable support, founder time, failed activation, or repeated explanation.',
  'Small SaaS: $2,000–$15,000/month when the same flow creates support load, onboarding drag, bad data, or qualification noise.',
  'Medium SaaS: $10,000–$75,000/month when product, support, customer success, onboarding, and implementation all carry the same missing scaffold.',
  'Larger mid-market systems: $50,000+/month when the failure affects activation, retention, implementation timelines, or multiple teams.',
];
---

<section id="cost-of-inaction">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Cost of Inaction</div>
    <h2>The cost is already being carried. Usually under the wrong line item.</h2>
    <p class="section-copy">
      A small or mid-sized SaaS does not need a Fortune 500 outage to lose money. It can lose it
      through recurring work: repeated tickets, extra onboarding calls, bad intake data, stalled
      activation, customer success workarounds, and the same internal debate every week.
    </p>
    <div class="project-card offer-card">
      <h3>Typical monthly cost ranges for unresolved guidance failure:</h3>
      <ul class="deliverable-list">
        {ranges.map((range) => <li>{range}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="section-copy cost-transfer-close">
      These ranges are not a promise. They are a way to ask the right question: is the recurring
      cost of not diagnosing the flow already higher than the cost of the review?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
